          dealing: '1. Remove the 4 Aces and place them in a vertical column in the center (The Foundations). 2. Deal the remaining 48 cards face up into 8 horizontal rows (Wings) on either side of the Aces (4 rows left, 4 rows right). Each row has 6 cards overlapping so only the end card is accessible.',
          hasPlaceholders: false,
        },
        rules: {
          objective: 'Build all four Foundation piles up from Ace to King in the same suit.',
          gameplay: '1. **Foundations**: Build UP by suit (e.g., 2♥ on A♥). 2. **Wings (Tableau)**: The top (exposed) card of any Wing is available for play. You can build DOWN on the wings regardless of suit (e.g., playing a 6♠ on a 7♦). 3. **Moving Cards**: Only one card can be moved at a time. 4. **Empty Spaces**: If a Wing becomes empty, you can fill the space with ANY available card (usually a King is best to start a new sequence).',
          keyRules: [
            'Any suit can be built on any suit in the tableau (unlike Solitaire/Klondike where it must be alternating colors).',
            'Only the top card is movable.',
            'Empty spaces are critical—creating one is usually the primary Short-Term Goal.',
          ],
          hasPlaceholders: false,
        },
        strategy: {
          basic: "Prioritize digging out the 2s and 3s. Since 2s are buried, you can't build your foundations. Look for moves that uncover low cards.",
          intermediate: "The 'Empty Slot' Gambit: Don't fill an empty wing space unless it helps you unblock another pile. An empty space is a 'virtual cell' that lets you shuttle cards around.",
          advanced: 'Reverse Building: Before moving a card to a foundation, check if you need it in the Tableau to hold a lower card. Sometimes keeping a 5 on the board allows you to stack a 4 on it, clearing space elsewhere.',
          tips: [
            'Plan 3 moves ahead.',
            "Don't build down a long sequence on a pile that hides a card you need immediately.",
          ],
          hasPlaceholders: false,
        },
